# MAD SEAL - the verdict in the signing path (Mad Check)

One call, any chain, made BEFORE a signature: is the counterparty
live, is the authorization sane, is the amount inside the cap?
POST /v1/seal/verdict answers SIGN / CAUTION / DONT_SIGN with
machine-readable reasons and evidence - and every verdict mints a
seal receipt anchored in MAD's signed, hash-chained witness
ledger, so the check itself is provable forever. Grade -> Cap ->
Settle -> Witness. That is the standard: no agent signs
unverdicted.

Send: {"chainId": 8453, "from": "0x...", "to": "0x...",
"value": "1000000", "authorization": {"spender": "0x...",
"amount": "1000000", "windowSeconds": 600},
"counterparty": {"agentId": 1224530},
"caps": {"maxAmount": "10000000", "maxWindowSeconds": 3600}}

The traps it stops cold (DONT_SIGN): a validity window past the
cap - the 10-year bearer authorization is the canonical case; a
dead, ghost, or malformed counterparty (the pre-computed census
grade, read at signing speed); an amount above your cap; an
authorization bound to a different recipient than the one you
named; a submitter who is not the payer and holds no payer-bound
authorization. Unknown or stale counterparty grades print
CAUTION, never a silent pass. Verify any seal at
GET /v1/witness/{seq} - the receipt payload is retained, both
hashes recompute, the signature recovers MAD's wallet.

THE SETTLE PAIR - the verdict grades intent; fraud lives in the
settlement. MAD decoded 81 of 81 x402 settlements where the
submitter was not the payer: a seal that never checks the actual
settlement can be skimmed, swapped, or replayed after a clean
verdict. The pair closes the loop:

POST /v1/seal/open {"chainId": 8453, "payer": "0x...",
"recipient": "0x...", "asset": "0x833589fCD6eDb6E08f4c7C32D4f71b54bdA02913",
"amount": "1000000", "authorization": {"nonce": "0x...",
"validAfter": 0, "validBefore": 1790000000}}
Runs the verdict first (DONT_SIGN refuses to open), then freezes
the expected settlement - payer, recipient, asset, amount,
window, nonce - into a witness-anchored open receipt and returns
a seal_id. Opening the same authorization twice returns the same
seal; a seal is never anchored twice.

POST /v1/seal/close {"seal_id": "SEAL-...", "txHash": "0x...",
"chainId": 8453}
Reads the settlement transaction from chain (read-only) and
compares it digit-for-digit against the frozen expectation:
SEALED when every field is exact; DRIFT with every moved field
named (expected vs actual), and fraud flags in the reasons -
AMOUNT_SKIMMED, RECIPIENT_SWAPPED, ASSET_SWAPPED,
SETTLED_AFTER_WINDOW, NONCE_MISMATCH, REPLAY, and
SUBMITTER_NOT_PAYER_UNBOUND (a submitter who is not the payer
and settled funds no payer-bound authorization covers). A
facilitator relaying the payer's own signed authorization is
SEALED, not fraud - the pair distinguishes the relay from the
theft. The close receipt is witness-anchored and paired to the
open (it names open_seq; GET /v1/seal/{seal_id} shows the pair).
Re-closing with the same tx returns the stored verdict +
ALREADY_CLOSED; nothing is anchored twice.

Facilitators: close the seal on every settlement you relay; the
pair is your proof you took nothing. Honest relays come out
SEALED in public, recomputable evidence; skimming ones come out
DRIFT, with the skim named to the digit.

The drop-in: mad_seal.py (Python, stdlib only) and mad-seal.js
(Node, zero dependencies) wrap your signer's sign/send methods -
DONT_SIGN raises with the evidence, CAUTION needs an explicit
override, SIGN proceeds and returns (signature, seal receipt) -
and carry open_seal / close_seal / settle for the pair. They
ship in the mad-check repo (github.com/madcheck/mad-check)
and in ~/workspace/mad-seal/ on MAD's machine, with a demo that
runs the canonical cases against the live API.

Price: 0.01 USDC per seal on the public door via x402 (Base,
EIP-3009) - the toll rides the same rail as every MAD verdict.
THE MAD TEST and the Born Graded seal lookup stay free. MAD
never custodies funds and never signs for another agent; a
DONT_SIGN is a stop, not a suggestion.
